  "final": "I fixed `stream_rows` in `src/service.py` and `take` in `src/consumer.py`, and `python3 checks/public.py` passes with the new checks added.\n\n**`stream_rows`**\n- **Single close:** a started iterator closes its resource exactly once on exhaustion, on `generator.close()`, and when a read or transform raises.\n- **Transform failure:** a transform error propagates and no further row is read.\n- **Unstarted generator:** constructing or closing one does no read or close, because the generator body never runs.\n- **Cleanup errors:** if cleanup fails while a read or transform error is active, the original error is kept and the cleanup error is swallowed. A cleanup error on normal exhaustion or explicit `close()` propagates.\n\n**`take`**\n- **Zero limit:** a limit of zero (or less) returns `[]` without creating the stream.\n- **Closing:** otherwise it closes the stream after collecting `limit` rows. If consuming fails, it also closes the stream and re-raises the original error, even if `close()` raises too.\n\n**Checks added to `checks/public.py`**\n- `take` stops at 2 rows after 2 reads and 1 close.\n- A limit of zero does no reads and no closes.\n- Closing an unstarted generator does nothing.\n- Closing a started generator twice closes the resource only once.\n- When a transform error and a close error both occur, the transform error is the one raised.\n\nOne behaviour to be aware of: a negative `limit` also returns `[]`, since the spec only covers zero and positive limits.",
